# Three Days in March: Crisis, Decision and Profound Relief

The period between 10 and 14 March 2025 should not be reduced to a routine interval between prescribing and dispensing medication. On the patient’s evidence, those days mark the point at which years of pain, treatment history, diminishing hope and unresolved clinical meaning converged into an acute existential crisis.

On 10 March 2025, the patient attended what became his final consultation with the treating neurologist. The clinical record describes the feet as his dominant problem. He felt increasingly “cornered” by Parkinson’s. He described “murder in the feet,” said that the first 150 footsteps were “torture,” and told the neurologist:

> “I won’t live with it.”

That statement did not emerge without warning. In 2023, the GP had already reported that the extreme burning pain was “causing death wish.” The same referral warned that the patient was not doing well despite his outward facade and requested review “before we get into trouble here.” Other records described the pain as psychologically consuming him and ordinary walking as a psychological mountain. By March 2025, the record therefore contained approximately two years of explicit death-related or grave psychological warning associated with the foot pain.

The patient recalls that the neurologist appeared nervous at the beginning of the 10 March consultation and failed to make eye contact. That evidence should be stated precisely. The patient observed the neurologist’s demeanour; he cannot directly prove the neurologist’s internal state. Nevertheless, the observation forms part of the circumstances in which the consultation occurred and may be assessed alongside what each participant knew.

The neurologist knew that Parkinson’s medication modified the feet. The March record expressly states that the patient knew “very well” that once Sinemet reached his system, his feet felt better from a pain perspective. The longer record contained earlier neurological modification through Procyclidine, deterioration when that medication was withdrawn, OFF-related foot and walking symptoms, later dopaminergic responses, and repeated treatment adjustments directed at the feet.

Sinemet controlled release was prescribed on 10 March for the nighttime and severe morning problem. The patient did not collect the medication until 13 March. He first woke after taking it on the morning of 14 March and experienced what the neurologist’s later complaint response described as:

> “Profound relief.”

The three-day delay requires the patient’s own explanation. It cannot responsibly be interpreted merely as non-compliance, reluctance or administrative inconvenience.

The patient had already told the neurologist that he was not willing to continue living with the condition as it then existed. Wrapped inside that statement, on his evidence, was the conviction that he would never see that neurologist again. The newly prescribed medication presented him with two possible futures, both of which were psychologically devastating.

If it failed, he believed that the last plausible avenue of relief had closed and that he had reached the end of what he was capable of enduring. If it succeeded, he would have to confront an entirely different catastrophe: that the feet had been neurologically treatable, that the treatment relationship had been modifying them for years, and that prolonged suffering might have occurred within a course which should have been reconsidered much earlier.

The delay between prescription and collection was therefore a period of decision under extreme psychological pressure. The patient was not deciding simply whether to try another tablet. He was deciding whether he could bear to learn which of those two realities he occupied.

When he woke on 14 March with profound relief, the result did more than improve a symptom. It reorganised the meaning of the preceding history. The relief connected the severe morning foot experience to a targeted nighttime dopaminergic intervention with an immediacy the patient could feel for himself. It was capable of changing his understanding of what had happened since 2017, what earlier medication responses had meant, and why the feet had continued to return throughout neurological care.

This does not mean that treatment response, standing alone, conclusively proves the sole mechanism of every component of the foot pain. Nor does it establish by itself that every earlier clinical judgment was negligent. Its evidential force lies elsewhere. The response was sufficiently real for the neurologist to acknowledge it as profound, and it occurred after a long course in which the feet had repeatedly shown neurological state-sensitivity. It therefore demanded more than prospective satisfaction that the current prescription was helping. It raised a retrospective question about the adequacy of the history under which treatment had been managed.

Five days after first waking with that relief, and nine days after the final consultation, the patient submitted his formal complaint. The proximity matters. The complaint was not written by a person calmly surveying a fully understood eight-year history. It was produced by a patient emerging from documented catastrophic pain, after an express statement that he would not live with it, and immediately after discovering that targeted nighttime medication could provide profound relief.

The complaint response subsequently stated:

> “I did not understand that this was how he felt.”

It also asserted:

> “In essence we have known that [Liam] has Parkinson’s; we have known it would be Sinemet or other dopamine agonist responsive.”

Those statements create the central knowledge asymmetry. The institution retrospectively claimed continuity of diagnostic and therapeutic knowledge. The patient, however, had not been living with knowledge that the disputed feet were being maintained as a separate or unresolved problem within an inherited management architecture. He believed that he had reported their medication responsiveness and that Neurology therefore knew what needed to be known.

Nor did the patient initially understand the documentary significance of the omitted 2017 Protest. He did not expect every bedside conversation to appear in a discharge summary. The omission became legally and institutionally meaningful only when the hospital relied upon the apparent continuity and completeness of the resulting record to rebut the complaint, without reconstructing the disputed beginning.

The knowledge acquired in March 2025 was therefore not necessarily knowledge of the originating omission, its possible purpose or its downstream documentary effect. It was the immediate and emotionally overwhelming knowledge that the medication had worked. The broader case architecture required access to records, comparison of their contents, recognition that the Protest was absent, and analysis of how the apparently uncontested discharge foundation had been inherited and later used defensively.

The proper formulation is:

> Between 10 and 14 March 2025, the patient moved from an expressly documented unwillingness to continue living with catastrophic pain to profound relief following targeted nighttime levodopa. The three-day delay in collecting the medication was part of that crisis, not a departure from it. The eventual response did not instantly disclose the whole case; it supplied the fact which compelled the patient to begin asking why a neurologically modifiable problem had governed so much of his life without the original foundation ever being visibly reconstructed.

Those days are therefore relevant to injury, credibility, causation, the timing and content of the complaint, and the patient’s later acquisition of knowledge. They are also human evidence. They show what the institutional history meant when it finally arrived, not as an abstract record problem, but in the consciousness of the person who had lived inside it.
